{
  "dom_id": "step-q85687",
  "name": "Question 18",
  "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/multivariable/14222582/assets/b0aa7d362596dcea976fead0467b9eb76c04b85c02c55dbd12e9a95652eda276.png)\n\nThe region enclosed by the curves $y=x$ and $y=\\sqrt{x}$ for $y\\in [0,1]$ is rotated $2\\pi $ radians about the line $x=3.$ Find the volume of the resulting solid.",
  "worked_solution": "First, since we are rotating about a vertical line, we need to solve the equations of our curves for $x:$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y=x & \\,⟹\\,x=y \\\\ y=\\sqrt{x} & \\,⟹\\,x={y}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let's draw a horizontal arrow through the given region. The arrow should be pointing away from the axis of rotation.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/multivariable/14222582/assets/f15165acc18f4cf828671e3f9085274b26598a643be9eeb97be617272d7008d7.png)\n\nThe horizontal arrow \n\n- *enters* the region through the inner curve $x=y,$ and\n\n- *leaves* through the outer curve $x={y}^{2}.$\n\nNext, we can find the volume of our solid as the difference between the volumes of the solids generated by the outer and inner curves.\n\n- **Step I.** Figure out the volume generated by the outer curve $x={y}^{2}.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/multivariable/14222582/assets/54820b3b386c9a435de04e0b3f07d4e2221a3e026ba5ba93ca25fa6640aa0ab1.png)\n\nThe corresponding volume is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{V}_{\\text{outer}} & =\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}[\\,(\\text{right})-(\\text{left})\\,]^{2}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}{[\\,(3)-({y}^{2})\\,]}^{2}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}{[3-{y}^{2}]}^{2}\\,\\text{d}y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- **Step II.** Figure out the volume generated by the inner curve $x=y.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/multivariable/14222582/assets/3e1a6067500f81cabeac8dc43c4c585da608ea655667dc5e00f29a6fad0dda60.png)\n\nThe corresponding volume is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{V}_{\\text{inner}} & =\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}[\\,(\\text{right})-(\\text{left})\\,]^{2}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}[\\,(3)-(y)\\,]^{2}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}[3-y]^{2}\\,\\text{d}y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- **Step III.** Find the difference.\n\nFinally, we can write the volume of the original solid as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V & ={V}_{\\text{outer}}-{V}_{\\text{inner}} \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}({(3-{y}^{2})}^{2}-{(3-y)}^{2})\\,\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}({y}^{4}-7{y}^{2}+6y)\\,\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\pi {(\\frac{{y}^{5}}{5}-\\frac{7{y}^{3}}{3}+3{y}^{2})|}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =\\pi [(\\frac{{1}^{5}}{5}-\\frac{7\\cdot {1}^{3}}{3}+3\\cdot {1}^{2})-0] \\\\  & =\\frac{13\\pi }{15}.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
